#: source/language/string-literals.md:1
msgid "String literals"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:3
msgid "A _string literal_ represents a [string](types.md#type-string) value."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:5
msgid "**Syntax**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:7
msgid "_expression_ → _string_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:9
msgid ""
"_string_ → `\"` ( _string_char_\\* [_interpolation_element_](./string-"
"interpolation.md) )\\* _string_char_\\* `\"`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:11
msgid ""
"_string_ → `''` ( _indented_string_char_\\* [_interpolation_element_](./"
"string-interpolation.md) )\\* _indented_string_char_\\* `''`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:13
msgid "_string_ → _uri_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:15
msgid "_string_char_ ~ `[^\"$\\\\]|\\$(?!\\{)|\\\\.`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:17
msgid ""
"_indented_string_char_ ~ `[^$']|\\$\\$|\\$(?!\\{)|''[$']|''\\\\.|'(?!')`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:19
msgid "_uri_ ~ `[A-Za-z][+\\-.0-9A-Za-z]*:[!$%&'*+,\\-./0-9:=?@A-Z_a-z~]+`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:21
msgid "Strings can be written in three ways."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:23
msgid ""
"The most common way is to enclose the string between double quotes, e.g., "
"`\"foo bar\"`. Strings can span multiple lines. The results of other "
"expressions can be included into a string by enclosing them in `${ }`, a "
"feature known as [string interpolation](./string-interpolation.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:29
msgid ""
"The following must be escaped to represent them within a string, by "
"prefixing with a backslash (`\\`):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:31
msgid "Double quote (`\"`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:33 source/language/string-literals.md:43
#: source/language/string-literals.md:53 source/language/string-literals.md:65
#: source/language/string-literals.md:106
#: source/language/string-literals.md:124
#: source/language/string-literals.md:136
#: source/language/string-literals.md:154
#: source/language/string-literals.md:170
msgid "**Example**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:35
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"\\\"\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:39
msgid ""
"    \"\\\"\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:41
msgid "Backslash (`\\`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:45
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"\\\\\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:49
msgid ""
"    \"\\\\\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:51
msgid ""
"Dollar sign followed by an opening curly bracket (`${`) – \"dollar-curly\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:55
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"\\${\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:59
msgid ""
"    \"\\${\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:61
msgid ""
"The newline, carriage return, and tab characters can be written as `\\n`, "
"`\\r` and `\\t`, respectively."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:63 source/language/string-literals.md:168
msgid "A \"double-dollar-curly\" (`$${`) can be written literally."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:67
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"$${\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:71
msgid ""
"    \"$\\${\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:73
msgid ""
"String values are output on the terminal with Nix-specific escaping. Strings "
"written to files will contain the characters encoded by the escaping."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:76
msgid ""
"The second way to write string literals is as an _indented string_, which is "
"enclosed between pairs of _double single-quotes_ (`''`), like so:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:86
msgid ""
"This kind of string literal intelligently strips indentation from the start "
"of each line. To be precise, it strips from each line a number of spaces "
"equal to the minimal indentation of the string as a whole (disregarding the "
"indentation of empty lines). For instance, the first and second line are "
"indented two spaces, while the third line is indented four spaces. Thus, two "
"spaces are stripped from each line, so the resulting string is"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:94
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"This is the first line.\\nThis is the second line.\\n  This is the third "
"line.\\n\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:98 source/language/string-literals.md:164
msgid "**Note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:100
msgid ""
"Whitespace and newline following the opening `''` is ignored if there is no "
"non-whitespace text on the initial line."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:102
msgid "**Warning**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:104
msgid "Prefixed tab characters are not stripped."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:108
msgid "The following indented string is prefixed with tabs:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:116
msgid ""
"    \"\\tall:\\n\\t\\t@echo hello\\n\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:118
msgid ""
"Indented strings support [string interpolation](./string-interpolation.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:120
msgid "The following must be escaped to represent them in an indented string:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:122
msgid "`$` is escaped by prefixing it with two single quotes (`''`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:132
msgid ""
"    \"$\\n\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:134
msgid "`''` is escaped by prefixing it with one single quote (`'`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:144
msgid ""
"    \"''\\n\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:146
msgid "These special characters are escaped as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:147
msgid "Linefeed (`\\n`): `''\\n`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:148
msgid "Carriage return (`\\r`): `''\\r`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:149
msgid "Tab (`\\t`): `''\\t`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:151
msgid "`''\\` escapes any other character."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:153
msgid "A \"dollar-curly\" (`${`) can be written as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:162
msgid ""
"    \"echo ${PATH}\\n\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:166
msgid ""
"This differs from the syntax for escaping a dollar-curly within double "
"quotes (`\"\\${\"`). Be aware of which one is needed at a given moment."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:178
msgid ""
"    \"$\\${\\n\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:180
msgid ""
"Indented strings are primarily useful in that they allow multi-line string "
"literals to follow the indentation of the enclosing Nix expression, and that "
"less escaping is typically necessary for strings representing languages such "
"as shell scripts and configuration files because `''` is much less common "
"than `\"`. Example:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:187
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"stdenv.mkDerivation {\n"
"...\n"
"postInstall =\n"
"  ''\n"
"    mkdir $out/bin $out/etc\n"
"    cp foo $out/bin\n"
"    echo \"Hello World\" > $out/etc/foo.conf\n"
"    ${if enableBar then \"cp bar $out/bin\" else \"\"}\n"
"  '';\n"
"...\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:201
msgid ""
"Finally, as a convenience, _URIs_ as defined in appendix B of [RFC 2396]"
"(http://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc2396.txt) can be written _as is_, without "
"quotes. For instance, the string `\"http://example.org/foo.tar.bz2\"` can "
"also be written as `http://example.org/foo.tar.bz2`."
msgstr ""
